[[:db/retract 17592186123206 :answer-field/choices 17592186123207] [:db/retract 17592186123206 :answer-field/choices 17592186123208] [:db/retract 17592186123206 :answer-field/choices 17592186123209] [:db/retract 17592186123206 :answer-field/choices 17592186123210] [:db/retract 17592186123206 :answer-field/choices 17592186123211] [:db/retract 17592186125108 :question/answer-fields 17592186123248] {:db/id 17592186124532 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-afc662b9-768c-5035-bd5f-1385f3afe28a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124341 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ for which $\\lambda_1=-1,$ $\\lambda_2=1,$ $\\lambda_3=0$ are eigenvalues, and\n![](images/10/10c3a1128f78cd19b14ca29bda23bcd3c14a49a6bbf2608aa568f6f7c22aea9b.svg)\nare the respective eigenvectors. What are the values of $a,b,c$ given that\n![](images/83/837556eba2375812c7d20c59533fc51f91716c6306254d2fc193b98315dd5663.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are $\\lambda_1={\\color{blue}-1},$ $\\lambda_2={\\color{red}1},$ $\\lambda_3=0,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n![](images/20/20ee95979c2ae9a6f3eca54e65923f608ef569141c6ff1393ef53e99636dda1a.svg)\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_1={\\color{blue}-1},$\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_2={\\color{red}1},$ and\n\n- the $3$rd column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_3=0.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n![](images/13/13f61ba37464f828258713594c973c4ab7c5c1d6aa50c2b482dcc2b444013686.svg)\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n![](images/69/69506c47b7fce7fd91eee135299db2920598325d4eb28611d758aeb5e3815489.svg)\n\nIn conclusion, we get $a=0,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=-1.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124342 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n![](images/3e/3e68233c9221816c1bd834d2260a162ec6d4cc5e063022c8b9e545948f703589.svg)\nDetermine whether $A=PDP^{-1}$ for some invertible matrix $P$ and a diagonal matrix $D,$ where $P$ and $D$ are both real. In the affirmative case, find a possible diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $\\mathbb R.$ \n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 6 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 6 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )([{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda +2]-12) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -10) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )(\\lambda -5)(\\lambda +2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\lambda_1=4,$ $\\lambda_2=5,$ and $\\lambda_3=-2.$ Since all of the eigenvalues are simple roots of the characteristic polynomial, $A$ is diagonalizable. \n\nWe construct a diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal. Therefore, one possible matrix $D$ is\n![](images/43/432aa48b4a3573c076a617dacc461cd5816c5279e79f9f14545609b0b4553f0c.svg)\n\nNote that the diagonal matrix $D$ is unique up to the ordering of the eigenvalues. Therefore, another possible  diagonal matrix is\n\n![](images/7c/7c7e458176039ee582b023ad53137c6adf5d66fdb7e17e2a62aedde6a160545a.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124534 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-938c5c1e-3528-5126-9a4c-fc692c630173"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124343 :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n![](images/95/95456befbc775262e58e48b5e32503b70e1a675d5c4b79911bfd3d526880d42e.svg)\nGiven that $D=P^{-1}AP,$ find the first column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are  \n\n$\\qquad$ $\\lambda_1=-2, \\quad$ $\\lambda_2=-1, \\quad$ $\\lambda_3=2.$ \n\nSo, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=-2.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda_1=-2.$ Computing $A-(-2)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -4 & 6 \\\\ -9 & -7 & 9 \\\\ -16 & -10 & 14\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 & 6 \\\\ -9 & -5 & 9 \\\\ -16 & -10 & 16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ that we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -9 & -5 & 9 & 0 \\\\ -16 & -10 & 16 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{8}{3}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{3} & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{2}{3}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, $x_3$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain $x_2=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get $x_1=x_3.$\nHence, the general solution is\n\n![](images/9a/9ac30b66a0fb5232b248e7ccf6d5b57267168e328387f233dfdb793fbb7ea287.svg)\n\nTherefore, setting $x_3=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n![](images/db/dbd8e786b6f8ffa50a35abc0136871f855f56fd4afff75c4ef6542dfa9b08f10.svg)\n\nFinally, ![](images/df/df31aa59467f4b7c37d2406d5ad6571a69baa6f9a7868f6bd868e45cf10c15aa.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124385 :question/problem "![](images/0a/0acea11970e0a11fd3d6df09c46335ca747b58d82e6cf5a3c1bdfe941ccae242.svg) \nThe matrix $A$ has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=2,$ $\\lambda_2=1,$ and $\\lambda_3=0.$\nFind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D=P^{-1}AP.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we are already given the eigenvalues of the matrix: \n\n$\\qquad$ $\\lambda_1=2,\\quad$ $\\lambda_2=1, \\quad$ $\\lambda_3=0.$ \n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ Applying our usual method, we get the following.\n\n- For $\\lambda_1=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives us the eigenvector ![](images/00/001911806161a251ef55b16759d586e6d6747a8d34bae00c07eb89c8179cde84.svg)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives us the eigenvector ![](images/41/413d4dbc71e9f4059fc86d85806e009d5d41dfd8a87b9ea159badcc6003602e5.svg)\n\n- For $\\lambda_3=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives us the eigenvector ![](images/18/187539cd8867c5b8592cf3b6f928992f7be756c25a9a641741ba98150072f307.svg)\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n![](images/a0/a09b2916af73b1d593ac237e5e895ff3d0c31d4978b66e4358dbd5f132547324.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-afc662b9-768c-5035-bd5f-1385f3afe28a" :question/id #uuid "afc662b9-768c-5035-bd5f-1385f3afe28a" :question/math-academy-id "q-83709" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ for which ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=-5$ are eigenvalues, and\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nare the respective eigenvectors. What are the values of $a,b,c$ given that\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}a & 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & b \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & c\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}a & 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & b \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=-5,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal: \n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ and\n\n- the $3$rd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{3}=-5.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, we get $a=-2,$ $b=0,$ and $c=-5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f7eb7acd-4f6f-5c51-b373-dbdb10113bb6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f7eb7acd-4f6f-5c51-b373-dbdb10113bb6" :answer-field/id #uuid "f7eb7acd-4f6f-5c51-b373-dbdb10113bb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5b6b9cd9-57fe-539e-8f9a-c6c105d80efa" :answer-field/choices ["answer-41f2343b-0ba1-541f-bfdd-50ec15ef6109" "answer-5b6b9cd9-57fe-539e-8f9a-c6c105d80efa" "answer-12b239ff-66d7-5a15-b91a-27cbd26aafcf" "answer-ba317e38-2bd5-592f-b3a3-98de785fc396" "answer-e835880d-6a15-5422-a117-36750218335a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-41f2343b-0ba1-541f-bfdd-50ec15ef6109" :answer/id #uuid "41f2343b-0ba1-541f-bfdd-50ec15ef6109" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a=-1,$ $b=0,$ and $c=5$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5b6b9cd9-57fe-539e-8f9a-c6c105d80efa" :answer/id #uuid "5b6b9cd9-57fe-539e-8f9a-c6c105d80efa" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a=-2,$ $b=0,$ and $c=-5$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-12b239ff-66d7-5a15-b91a-27cbd26aafcf" :answer/id #uuid "12b239ff-66d7-5a15-b91a-27cbd26aafcf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a=-5,$ $b=2,$ and $c=0$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ba317e38-2bd5-592f-b3a3-98de785fc396" :answer/id #uuid "ba317e38-2bd5-592f-b3a3-98de785fc396" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a=1,$ $b=2,$ and $c=5$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e835880d-6a15-5422-a117-36750218335a" :answer/id #uuid "e835880d-6a15-5422-a117-36750218335a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a=2,$ $b=1,$ and $c=5$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125101 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ for which ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1$ are eigenvalues, and \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nare the respective eigenvectors. Find the matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=A,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix." :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ and\n\n- the $3$rd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186123206 :answer-field/correct "answer-2103dd17-24f4-5247-b56e-72b117146a35" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6054a99d-5ddb-5221-9dce-cc7c44c1f9ca" "answer-2103dd17-24f4-5247-b56e-72b117146a35" "answer-1d28198c-10ed-558f-8e71-d5f2fb79b590" "answer-2efa584d-3db2-57e9-a646-6fda0b51e92f" "answer-b4acffba-1ec8-59df-8d94-4699791de74a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6054a99d-5ddb-5221-9dce-cc7c44c1f9ca" :answer/id #uuid "6054a99d-5ddb-5221-9dce-cc7c44c1f9ca" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2103dd17-24f4-5247-b56e-72b117146a35" :answer/id #uuid "2103dd17-24f4-5247-b56e-72b117146a35" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1d28198c-10ed-558f-8e71-d5f2fb79b590" :answer/id #uuid "1d28198c-10ed-558f-8e71-d5f2fb79b590" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2efa584d-3db2-57e9-a646-6fda0b51e92f" :answer/id #uuid "2efa584d-3db2-57e9-a646-6fda0b51e92f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b4acffba-1ec8-59df-8d94-4699791de74a" :answer/id #uuid "b4acffba-1ec8-59df-8d94-4699791de74a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-938c5c1e-3528-5126-9a4c-fc692c630173" :question/id #uuid "938c5c1e-3528-5126-9a4c-fc692c630173" :question/math-academy-id "q-40161" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & ∗ \\\\ 0 & 1 & ∗ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $D={P}^{-1}AP,$ find the third column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=4.$ \n\nSo, the third column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}=4.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{3}=4.$ Computing $A-(4)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{3}I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]-(4)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations whose augmented matrix $M$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above matrix is already  in row echelon form.  It has two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable.  From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}={x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{2}(-4({x}_{3})-6{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =5{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f4aa1985-d36d-5245-9e3f-b2f24a2cdea0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f4aa1985-d36d-5245-9e3f-b2f24a2cdea0" :answer-field/id #uuid "f4aa1985-d36d-5245-9e3f-b2f24a2cdea0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ef768124-15c8-5064-8cfc-be6969ca32f7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-94406db6-bcd3-5a97-9f39-575286530355" "answer-78bcd352-9548-567a-a122-5893ecb2b938" "answer-0ea81202-7482-5812-86cb-8eb7218d7d61" "answer-b8490153-50bf-57a4-8199-293216881d4d" "answer-ef768124-15c8-5064-8cfc-be6969ca32f7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-94406db6-bcd3-5a97-9f39-575286530355" :answer/id #uuid "94406db6-bcd3-5a97-9f39-575286530355" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-78bcd352-9548-567a-a122-5893ecb2b938" :answer/id #uuid "78bcd352-9548-567a-a122-5893ecb2b938" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0ea81202-7482-5812-86cb-8eb7218d7d61" :answer/id #uuid "0ea81202-7482-5812-86cb-8eb7218d7d61" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b8490153-50bf-57a4-8199-293216881d4d" :answer/id #uuid "b8490153-50bf-57a4-8199-293216881d4d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ef768124-15c8-5064-8cfc-be6969ca32f7" :answer/id #uuid "ef768124-15c8-5064-8cfc-be6969ca32f7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125792 :tutorial/content "An $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ is said to be diagonalizable if there exists a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that \n\n$$\nA=PDP^{-1}.\n$$\n\n \n\nFor example, the matrix ![](images/81/810ae901dcd83f59297a122fbf20beb91e29374baff41c19bcacf40b3a7e7091.svg) is diagonalizable because\n![](images/2c/2c41221da0e4c0895928e65602e588f380452172e29d79f6e9f68ebb49e57807.svg)\n\nIn general, we have the following theorem:\n\n*An $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ is diagonalizable if and only if there exists a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ that consists of eigenvectors of $A$.*\n\nSince eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues are linearly independent, we can also  conclude the following fact:\n\n*If an $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ has $n$ *distinct* eigenvalues (that is, $n$ simple roots of the corresponding characteristic equation), then there exists a basis of eigenvectors. Thus, the matrix is diagonalizable.*\n\nLet's show you how to use the diagonalization theorem with an example. \n\nSuppose that $\\lambda_1={\\color{blue}-2},$ $\\lambda_2={\\color{red}1},$ $\\lambda_3=-3$ are eigenvalues of a $3\\times 3$  matrix $A$, and that\n![](images/ec/ecc57b51a49c8d1d6fbadd69908dbf6f48803cd6ca1f72ad189a1e5410c5d71c.svg)\nare the respective eigenvectors. To find an invertible matrix $P$ and  a diagonal matrix $D$ such that $PDP^{-1} = A,$  we do the following: .\n\nFirst, we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n![](images/4f/4f878ce6a4b6f770cd520e22a8e4a3c6f8b3012b7de446bd3f533e61bda85a13.svg)\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_1={\\color{blue}-2},$\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_2={\\color{red}1},$ and\n\n- the $3$rd column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_3=-3.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n![](images/95/95c896911a6d54ea6385821846a3f0e4fc1a8f05f29e83b951ba270a3898938e.svg)\n\n**Note:** If the matrix $A$ does not have $n$ distinct eigenvalues, it is not always possible to diagonalize it. However, the algorithm to diagonalize any diagonalizable $n\\!\\times\\! n$ matrix is the same as the $3\\!\\times\\! 3$ case!" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124755 :activity/expected-seconds 1200.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
